SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Thompson♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Three months fighting a VA algorithm that labeled me "low priority" and nobody could explain why. HHS is about to hand American medicine to tech corporations that will never leave once they're inside.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Hall♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔American Society of Anesthesiologists♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Society of Anesthesiologists, we urge HHS to prioritize patient trust and human accountability before accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Thompson♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine welcomes this opportunity to respond, and urges HHS to require prospective real-world validation data before scaling clinical AI deployment, given persistent and well-documented gaps between benchmark performance and actual clinical outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔King♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔American Foundation for the Blind♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Foundation for the Blind urges HHS to establish clear accountability standards before accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, as existing frameworks leave harmed patients without recourse.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔White♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My youngest daughter spent almost three years being told she was anxious, or growing too fast, or just needed to stretch more. The specialist in Albuquerque who finally figured out what was wrong with her connective tissue did it because he actually read her chart the night before she came in. He remembered what he read. He looked at her when she walked through the door. No algorithm trained on massive datasets is ever going to duplicate that kind of intuition, and it makes zero sense to me why HHS is in such a hurry to build a system where those vital human connections are replaced by automation. The thing that made her care trustworthy was knowing a specific human being was responsible. Please slow this down and protect that before it's gone from medicine entirely, because once it's gone I don't think you get it back.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Anderson♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔American Hospital Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Hospital Association⏎155 N. Wacker Drive⏎Chicago, IL 60606⏎⏎October 15, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎The American Hospital Association submits this comment on behalf of its member hospitals and health systems. The question the Department has still not resolved is straightforward: if an AI tool makes an error and a patient gets hurt, exactly where does the liability rest? Not in theory. In practice, with a name attached.⏎⏎AHA members have sat in meetings where that question moves from the vendor to the hospital to the physician and back again until the meeting ends and nobody has answered it.⏎⏎Do not accelerate adoption without a clear, enforceable accountability structure in place first. The Department should establish that standard before any further adoption incentives are issued.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Hall♔♔California♔♔United States♔Duke University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Duke University School of Medicine respectfully submits that HHS cannot responsibly accelerate AI adoption in clinical care before democratic institutions have answered the prior question of how much AI medicine should contain.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔White♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Carol White, I retired last year after thirty-one years on the cardiology floor at Robert Wood Johnson in New Brunswick. Before HHS moves to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, I want to ask one question nobody seems to be asking: what happens when the nurses who catch the algorithm's mistakes are gone and there's nobody left to push back on what the screen says.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Perez♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔AstraZeneca♔♔♔♔♔♔AstraZeneca submits these comments to register serious concern that accelerating AI clinical deployment before real-world performance gaps are resolved repeats documented health IT failure patterns HHS should already recognize.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Jones♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔Endocrine Society♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Endocrine Society and the network of clinicians we represent, we urge HHS to pause and reconsider the framing of this request entirely. The question being asked is how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. We believe the prior question, the one that must be answered first, is what gets lost when the human relationship at the center of medicine is subordinated to computational efficiency.⏎⏎⏎⏎A patient sitting across from a clinician is not producing data. That patient is frightened, often unable to say what is actually wrong, and relying on another human being to notice what remains unspoken. No model processes that.⏎⏎⏎⏎The Endocrine Society asks HHS to reframe this RFI around precaution and human-centered values rather than speed of deployment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Jackson♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My last year in the cardiac unit at Iowa Methodist, I watched a seventy-three-year-old Grundy County farmer spend three days chasing a diagnosis that a flagging system kept missing because it simply had not been trained on men like him. That should matter to this agency. What concerns me about this request for information is the framing itself, which treats acceleration as the goal and oversight as something to fit in afterward. Once AI is embedded in billing codes and reimbursement structures, no hospital system removes it. That is not speculation; no major health IT system in this country ever has been unwound once the financial incentives locked in. The question of how much AI belongs in a clinical encounter is a moral question that patients and the public deserve to answer through deliberation, not one HHS should foreclose by moving faster than the governance structures exist to manage. I am asking the Department to slow this process down, build the accountability mechanisms first, and then ask the public whether this is the medicine they actually want.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Mitchell♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin Mitchell⏎4417 Magnolia Trace Drive⏎Baton Rouge, Louisiana 70808⏎⏎March 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎I printed this RFI to read at the reference desk where I work. It reminded me of when my wife Elaine got sick in 2022. We had to go to three different clinics for her stomach pain before we saw Dr. Carolyn Tureaud at Our Lady of the Lake. She just let Elaine talk, and figured out the issue mostly by noticing her posture in the chair.⏎⏎Your document makes it sound like you want to use AI to speed up doctor visits. When people are sick, they are scared and have a hard time explaining things. A computer program is just looking at typed text. It won't notice if someone is wincing or anxious. Please do not rely on computer systems to do a doctor's job.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Kevin Mitchell♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Martinez♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly I am not even sure this is the right place to send this but somebody shared the link on Facebook and said HHS was asking for input on AI in hospitals and doctors offices so here I am. My buddy Carlos, we been on the same crew out of Mesa going on fifteen years, ended up in the ICU after a clinic computer told him he was fine. What gets me is not the machine being wrong so much as nobody questioned it. The way I see it, when companies find a tool that cuts costs, they use it to cut costs, not to help workers like us. HHS should slow down and put real limits on this before⏎⏎Page 1 of 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Hernandez♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Equity in Healthcare Coalition♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Equity in Healthcare Coalition, representing more than 1,200 member organizations, this comment focuses on the sequencing question the RFI does not ask directly: whether acceleration is the right posture at all. A 2024 survey of our membership found that 68% had received patient complaints tied to AI-assisted clinical tools, with 74% of those complaints originating from low-income, rural, or racially minoritized patients.⏎⏎Equity in Healthcare Coalition Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎Fewer than one in three member organizations had any informed-consent process specifically addressing AI-driven recommendations. The concern here is not that AI lacks potential uses in clinical care. The concern is that embedding these tools into reimbursement structures before governance frameworks exist creates a one-way door.⏎⏎Once reimbursement incentives are aligned, reversing course becomes practically impossible regardless of what evidence emerges later about disparate harm.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Rivera♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Temple University Lewis Katz School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Lewis Katz School of Medicine's health economics working group has reviewed this RFI with considerable concern. Accelerating AI adoption without mandatory, population-stratified equity impact assessments is not a neutral policy choice. It is an affirmative decision to concentrate the greatest risks of AI failure on the populations HHS is legally obligated to protect.⏎⏎HHS must condition any AI acceleration initiative on completed equity assessments as a prerequisite, not an afterthought applied once deployment has already occurred in under-resourced settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Thomas♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, ACOG urges HHS to require meaningful human review of AI-driven coverage denials before accelerating adoption. Elderly, disabled, and non-English-speaking patients bear documented harms that never appear in appeal statistics because most patients never appeal.⏎⏎HHS is making a foreseeable choice.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Nguyen♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-four years of nursing, the last ten at Hennepin Healthcare in Minneapolis, and the case I keep returning to is a patient I'll call Mrs. Kowalski, a woman with a rare clotting disorder that two separate computer-generated screening tools both missed clean. Her profile didn't look like the patients those systems had learned from, and neither tool gave any indication it was struggling. No flag, no warning, nothing. It was a night-shift nurse who knew her personally, who noticed something felt wrong, who caught it before things turned critical. What worries me about this RFI is that I've watched health IT go through this same cycle before, with EHR rollouts, with sepsis algorithms, with alert fatigue that got so bad nurses started ignoring warnings that actually mattered. The tools work beautifully in demonstrations and fall apart when records are messy and incomplete, which is always. My ask is simple: require real-world performance evidence before deployment, and don't let the speed of adoption outpace the human judgment that still catches what the system♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Scott♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent three years getting the wrong answers before an AI tool finally caught something her doctors had missed. I soared when it happened. But I also knew the flag was right only because I had read every page of her chart myself — and most families don't have that.⏎⏎That experience is why I'm responding to this RFI, specifically on the question of what standards should exist BEFORE clinical AI tools are deployed at scale. Because right now, there are basically none that address bias in any meaningful way, and I think that is a serious problem.⏎⏎Here is what I keep coming back to. Sjoding et al. published a study in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 showing that pulse oximeters overestimate oxygen saturation in Black patients at nearly three times the rate they do in white patients. This bias persisted undetected for DECADES. When that oximetry data got fed into AI-assisted clinical decision tools during COVID-19, the error didn't disappear — it got amplified, because the system treated the flawed readings as reliable input. That is not a hypothetical risk. That is a documented, peer-reviewed harm that happened at scale before anyone was looking for it.⏎⏎And it's not an isolated case. Sepsis prediction algorithms have been shown to under-score Black patients, meaning they receive escalated intervention later than white patients with comparable presentations. Prior authorization AI tools used by insurers have denied claims at measurably higher rates for Black and Latino patients than for white patients with identical or similar diagnoses. These aren't anecdotes. They are documented patterns.⏎⏎What concerns me most about this RFI is that the framing is "how do we accelerate adoption" when the question should be "how do we audit what's already out there." Speed is being treated as the goal. It shouldn't be.⏎⏎AI systems trained on historical healthcare data inherit the inequities in that data. A system trained on records from a healthcare system that systematically undertreated Black patients, or that collected worse data on women's pain, or that had thinner records on rural populations — that system will reproduce those gaps. And because the output comes with a computational authority that feels objective, clinicians and administrators are LESS likely to push back than they would be with a human colleague's recommendation.⏎⏎My concrete ask to HHS is this: before any AI tool is deployed at scale in clinical or insurance settings, there should be mandatory independent bias auditing with public reporting of results. Not self-certification by the vendor. Independent auditing, with results accessible to the public and to patients. The audit should test performance across race, gender, age, and socioeconomic status at minimum.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to AI in healthcare. What happened for my mother was real. But "accurate on average" is not the same thing as safe for the patients who have always been treated as afterthoughts by the system these algorithms were trained on.⏎⏎The auditing infrastructure needs to exist FIRST. Not as a future priority after deployment, not as a voluntary framework that vendors can ignore if the compliance costs are inconvenient.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Mitchell♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Health insurance plans are currently using automated software to issue blanket coverage denials. It is physically impossible for a real doctor to be reviewing patient charts at the speed these rejections are sent out. In my 40 years as a nurse in Nebraska, I have seen medical technology used correctly—like a monitor flagging an irregular rhythm as a backup for the clinical team. But using algorithms to bypass human oversight and deny care is entirely different.⏎When claims are automatically rejected, the burden falls on patients who are already sick. Elderly and low-income individuals usually do not have the stamina to navigate a complicated appeals process just to get the care they were prescribed.⏎The Department of Health and Human Services must require strict human oversight for these processes. If a treatment is denied, a medical professional needs to put their actual name on that rejection. Insurers cannot be allowed to expand these automated systems without mandating individual human accountability for every denied claim.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Jones♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔Epic Systems Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher Jones, MD⏎⏎Chief Medical Officer, Regional Operations⏎⏎Epic Systems Corporation⏎⏎Providence, RI 02903⏎⏎⏎⏎August 14, 2023⏎⏎⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎⏎Epic Systems Corporation submits these comments regarding equity validation requirements during pre-deployment review. Observations across health systems utilizing clinical decision support (CDS) tools indicate a pattern that HHS must evaluate prior to finalizing any reimbursement expansion. Retrospective analyses identifying racial disparities in hardware such as pulse oximeters highlight significant vulnerabilities in clinical technology deployment broadly. Furthermore, independent external validation studies of predictive algorithms—including recent retrospective analyses of proprietary sepsis prediction models [1]—have demonstrated that algorithmic performance is highly sensitive to local population demographics and varying clinical workflows. Epic recognizes that these external analyses underscore a fundamental reality of machine learning: demographic and operational variances frequently remain latent until algorithms are deployed in a live production environment, necessitating rigorous local tuning.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS must balance the objective of reducing administrative friction against the patient safety risks inherent in accelerated implementation frameworks that lack mandated bias auditing. We recommend that the Department condition any proposed reimbursement incentives or regulatory pathway benefits on the completion of demonstrated equity validation prior to deployment. Such validation results must be disclosed to contracting health care organizations at the point of procurement, thereby preventing situations where retrospective review is only initiated following an adverse patient event.⏎⏎⏎⏎[1] Wong A, et al. External Validation of a Widely Implemented Proprietary Sepsis Prediction Model. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070.⏎⏎⏎⏎christopher.jones@epic.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Mitchell♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔Providence Health & Services♔♔♔♔♔♔I am writing on behalf of Providence Health & Services in Montana regarding the proposed AI certification framework. As a non-profit health system, we are worried about how these tools are being rolled out. We work with various technology vendors and commercial payers, and we keep seeing third-party developers focusing on bottom-line overhead cuts while ignoring patient care. HHS needs to establish clear rules here. Any certification framework must include workforce impact assessments and independent oversight. Otherwise, these commercial AI products will just create more administrative hurdles for the frontline staff who have to manage the daily disruptions of new software.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Johnson♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔American Society of Clinical Oncology♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO), HHS should not accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings until clear liability frameworks exist that identify a responsible party when AI-assisted decisions cause patient harm.⏎⏎The fundamental point is this: when something goes wrong, patients need someone who will answer.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Jones♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eight months being told she was low risk by whatever algorithm her primary care doctor was relying on, and she died of pancreatic cancer that a specialist in Burlington caught only after it was too late to do much. I think about that every single day when I'm with Ruth, my 71-year-old neighbor whose chart I've started reading because nobody else seems to be connecting the dots for her. What I want HHS to understand is that the thing medicine runs on is not data, it's trust, and trust comes from a person who will look you in the eye and take responsibility for what happens to you. Even when an algorithm gets the answer technically right, if it pulled the clinician's attention away from what Ruth's voice sounds like when she's scared, something real got lost. Please, slow this down and make sure a human being is always the one who answers for the outcome.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Lewis♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔National Immigration Law Center♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The National Immigration Law Center urges HHS to require meaningful human review before any AI-generated coverage denial takes effect. Accelerating adoption without this protection will deepen harm to elderly, disabled, and non-English-speaking patients who cannot navigate appeals.⏎⏎HHS inaction here is itself a policy choice, and the Department should name it as such.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Williams♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My buddy Hank got sent home with antacids by a computer in Minot last winter and nearly died. Don't let the people profiting from this write the rules about it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Green♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Emdeon♔♔♔♔♔♔Change Healthcare (formerly Emdeon) is submitting these comments in response to the HHS Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical settings. As a national healthcare technology company managing administrative and clinical data exchange, we are seeing deployment timelines speed up in ways that create real patient safety and regulatory vulnerabilities. Overall performance data for new machine learning models usually shows good initial accuracy, but serious operational risks emerge when these tools are applied to patient groups not well represented in the original training data. In our work with prior authorization and clinical decision support, we notice providers sometimes trust the AI too much and stop performing their own secondary checks. Disparities in algorithms used to predict readmissions or manage care enrollment often stay hidden until the software is rolled out across an entire health system.⏎Our primary goal here is to address how to catch this bias earlier. We strongly recommend that HHS create formal rules for how these systems are deployed. Required audits for bias need to be a strict condition for ONC certification or CMS accreditation, rather than just voluntary guidance. During recent advisory sessions with our clients, it became clear that confusion over liability with software not classified as a medical device is a major roadblock to adoption. Setting clear rules for testing before deployment would help clear up this legal uncertainty for both technology vendors and covered entities.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Harris♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Denise got misdiagnosed twice at our local clinic before a specialist down in Birmingham finally figured out what was actually wrong with her, and that was with real human doctors spending real time with her records. So when I read that HHS wants to speed up AI adoption in hospitals and clinics, my first thought is honestly just, who does this actually help. Because I've managed an office long enough to know how these decisions get made inside big organizations. When a hospital system or an insurance company sees a way to cut payroll by replacing a nurse coordinator or a billing reviewer with software, they take it, and then they write a press release about improved efficiency. The workers who lose those jobs are regular people. The savings go somewhere else. And if the AI gets it wrong, the patient is the one who pays for that, not whoever approved the contract.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Wright♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔After putting in decades of heavy construction work around West Virginia, my body has plenty of wear and tear. At 76, I spend more time at the doctor than I'd like. Recently my cardiology clinic up in Morgantown started using an AI program to pre-read EKGs. When I asked the doctor how the software makes its decisions, she couldn't really explain it. That is very concerning to me, and I also want to know who else is getting access to my medical records through this system.⏎⏎I just don't trust machines with my healthcare. My husband was misdiagnosed for two years. It took an actual doctor sitting down and looking at his history to finally figure it out. If these new AI systems are trained on old medical data that has mistakes in it, they are just going to repeat those exact same errors. Look at what happened with pulse oximeters giving incorrect oxygen readings for Black patients because the devices weren't tested on everyone. HHS needs to make sure any AI used in a clinic is strictly tested across all patient demographics.⏎⏎Another issue is insurance companies using algorithms to automatically deny our claims. You can't argue with a computer program. HHS has to force insurers to prove a real human reviewed the file before a denial. They also need to provide the reason in plain English so patients actually have a fair chance to appeal.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Gonzalez♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔Inovalon♔♔♔♔♔♔Inovalon submits these comments urging HHS to require documented workforce impact assessments before any administrative AI deployment receives reimbursement consideration, because what we have observed across health system partners is that labor savings consistently flow upward while frontline workers, disproportionately women and workers of color, bear the displacement.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Anderson♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My patient Mr. Delgado spent three extra days in the Bronx-Lebanon because an algorithm dismissed his chest pain. When something like that happens again, and it will, nobody will be left holding the bag.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Campbell♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔American Liver Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔American Liver Foundation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎American Liver Foundation submits this comment to state plainly that HHS is moving to embed AI into reimbursement structures before any independent governance framework exists, and that decision cannot be undone.⏎⏎American Liver Foundation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Johnson♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔i am writing about this HHS rule for AI in hospitals. i drove a truck out of kansas for almost 40 years and one thing I know is when the government or a boss says hurry up we will fix the details later... it means a disaster is coming.⏎i'm 72 now and dealing with some heart issues, im at the clinic a lot. A while back they put my whole file into some new computer portal system. It was a mess, it kept auto-scheduling me for stress tests I didnt need, the actual doctor was getting mad because it was wasting his time. And it missed a bad drug combo. I was on a blood pressure pill and they added another one that makes your pressure drop too low if you take them together. The computer system said it was fine. I actually felt dizzy and sick for almost two weeks before the guy at the local pharmacy noticed the two bottles when I went in for a refill and told me to stop. So much for the smart machine.⏎we used electronic logs and GPS in the trucks toward the end of my career. The GPS would sometimes tell you to turn down a dirt road or say a bridge was safe when it wasnt. you still had to use your own eyes because the computer doesnt drive the rig. I just dont trust a machine to make actual medical decisions.⏎what really bothers me is HHS rushing to pay for this before its tested. Once Medicare starts handing out money for these AI programs, how do we ever stop it? Once a hospital buys a million dollar system and fires half their staff to save money... they are never going back to the old way. They sign these big contracts and then we are stuck with it. Even if patients are getting hurt. The hospital will just hire lobbyists to say it costs too much to change back. Regular folks like me dont have lobbyists in Washington.⏎so if the AI gets something wrong, how exactly do we pull the plug?? HHS needs to answer that. How does Medicare stop paying for it? they havent even finished the basic safety rules yet so why are they rushing the money part. Nobody asked the patients. I want a real doctor looking at my chart, I dont want to be lumped in by an algorithm. I want to know who gave HHS the right to make this permanent decision without asking the public first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Davis♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Aetna♔♔♔♔♔♔Aetna respectfully submits that HHS cannot accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings without first establishing who bears legal responsibility when an AI-assisted decision harms a patient.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Baker♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist retired two years ago and the new practice flagged me as "low risk" using some automated tool, despite 30 years of military service and exposure history that MATTERS. The nurse couldn't explain it. Nobody could. Somewhere an executive is counting what they saved by not having a person review my file, and I'm the one who gets the wrong recommendation. HHS needs to SLOW DOWN and put real limits on using AI to cut clinical staff or override judgment. Someone with an ACTUAL LICENSE needs to be responsible for what happens to my health.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Adams♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔American Diabetes Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Diabetes Association | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎On behalf of the American Diabetes Association, we urge HHS to condition any AI reimbursement or certification support on independent workforce impact assessments, not vendor self-reporting.⏎⏎American Diabetes Association | HHS AI RFI | Page 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Torres♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔COMMENT OF BRIAN TORRES, CASPER, WYOMING⏎Submitted in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎I. MY EXPERIENCE WITH CLINICAL AI, AND WHY IT MATTERS HERE⏎⏎My cardiologist in Casper ran my symptoms through a decision-support tool before ordering the stress test that found a blocked artery. That tool probably saved my life. I say that not to be dramatic but because I want whoever reads this to know I am not someone who distrusts AI in medicine. I have seen what it can do when it is pointed at the right problem.⏎⏎The problem I am writing about is a different use of the same technology entirely.⏎⏎⏎II. TWO VERY DIFFERENT USES OF THE SAME TOOL⏎⏎The AI that flagged my cardiac risk was aimed at finding something wrong so a doctor could fix it. The AI now being used by insurers in prior authorization decisions is aimed at finding reasons to say no. These are not the same thing, and HHS should not treat them as the same thing when writing policy about accelerating adoption.⏎⏎ProPublica and STAT News reported extensively on UnitedHealth Group's NaviHealth nH Predict algorithm, which was used to deny post-acute care to Medicare Advantage patients at rates that far exceeded what human reviewers would have approved. Elderly patients who needed skilled nursing care after hospitalizations were cut off based on an algorithm's prediction, with minimal physician involvement in the denial. Some of those patients or their families appealed. Most did not. Most people don't.⏎⏎⏎III. WHO GETS HURT AND WHY WE DON'T SEE IT⏎⏎The populations absorbing the most harm from AI-driven coverage denials are, roughly:⏎⏎1. Elderly Medicare Advantage enrollees who are already exhausted from an illness and don't have the energy to file appeals⏎2. People who don't speak English as a first language and can't parse a denial letter written in insurance language⏎3. Low-income patients who cannot take time off work to make phone calls and navigate an appeals process designed to be difficult⏎⏎The harm is invisible in aggregate data precisely because most people do not appeal. They just go without the care. That is not a system working correctly. That is a system designed so that the cost of fighting back is higher than most people can pay.⏎⏎⏎IV. WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE ACCELERATING ANYTHING⏎⏎HHS has the authority to act here. Choosing not to use that authority while simultaneously promoting AI adoption in insurance functions is not a neutral decision. I'd ask HHS to treat the following as non-negotiable before any acceleration agenda moves forward:⏎⏎o Mandatory human physician review of any AI-generated coverage denial before it becomes final⏎o Plain-language explanation requirements so a patient actually understands why they were denied⏎o Enforceable appeal rights with real timelines, not timelines that exist on paper but stretch months in practice⏎⏎I spent forty years reading structural blueprints. The difference between a building that stands and one that collapses is usually in the inspection requirements somebody decided weren't worth paying for. The oversight question here is the same question. Who is checking the algorithm's work, and what happens to the patient when nobody is?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Thomas♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MY DAUGHTER'S RECORDS GOT MIXED UP TWICE⏎⏎Renee spent almost three weeks going back and forth between urgent care and her regular doctor before anyone figured out her fatigue and joint pain were lupus. Three weeks. And the thing that finally cracked it open wasn't a test or a system flag. It was a doctor who sat with her long enough to notice she was scared and exhausted and had been dismissed too many times already. I keep thinking about what would have happened if that doctor had been leaning on a software recommendation instead of on his own judgment.⏎⏎Here is what worries me about this proposal. We already had a situation where Renee's records got confused between two clinics, and nobody caught the problem until a pharmacist in Wilmington noticed that the medication she was nearly prescribed would have badly flared her condition. A human being caught that. Someone who knew to stop and ask a question. If the current system, with real people responsible at every step, still produced that kind of near-miss, I genuinely do not understand why the answer is to add more software and move faster.⏎⏎WHAT I AM ASKING HHS TO DO⏎⏎Before any acceleration happens, there needs to be a real requirement that a doctor, not a program, is the one responsible when something goes wrong. I'm not saying AI can't help. Part of me honestly wishes something had flagged lupus for Renee back in those early weeks when she was bouncing around getting nowhere. But wishing for faster diagnosis and trusting a system to replace human judgment are two very different things. Any parent who has watched their child nearly get the wrong treatment because of a record error is going to feel the same way. Put the accountability rules in place first. Make sure there is always a specific person who can be asked♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Hill♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Families USA♔♔♔♔♔♔Families USA | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎Families USA submits this comment with one concern at the center: HHS is being asked to accelerate AI adoption before the public has had any real say in whether this is the direction medicine should go. Our member survey data shows 67% of practices lack basic audit protocols for bias. Once AI is embedded in reimbursement structures, reversing course becomes politically impossible.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Lewis♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔When my daughter Caitlin was twelve we drove 40 minutes to Omaha to see a specialist because our local clinic was so understaffed they almost missed her thyroid problem. The specialist didn't just look at her labs. He actually watched her sitting in the exam room and asked her about her 7th grade classes because he noticed she looked exhausted. A computer program isn't going to catch something like that. ⏎⏎I worry HHS is moving way too fast on adopting AI without thinking about how patients feel. Even if a computer spits out the right diagnosis, if there isn't a real person taking responsibility for it, it doesn't really feel like healthcare. I deal with automated systems at my customer service job and people hate them. HHS needs to make sure evaluations of these AI tools check if patients are interacting with a human who is accountable if a mistake happens.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Martin♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist told me in February that an AI tool caught something in my echocardiogram she might have missed. I am grateful. But grateful for a useful tool is not the same as agreeing to make it permanent before anyone has set the rules.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Taylor♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I almost had a major heart attack because of one of these computer programs. My cardiologist only mentioned it by accident during my checkup. Apparently some algorithm decided my stress test wasn't a priority and put me at the bottom of the list. I had to practically yell at the office staff to get my echocardiogram scheduled. When they finally did it four months later? Bad blockage. I needed a procedure right away.⏎⏎I'm 70 now and spent over four decades teaching elementary school here in New Jersey. You learn a lot about human nature doing that. When you put a machine in charge, people stop paying attention. That's exactly what happened with my doctor. He's a careful guy normally, but the computer said I was low priority so he just went along with it. We didn't even know the machine was making the call until I started asking questions. Doctors are just going to get lazy if they think the computer already checked the scans.⏎⏎Now HHS is trying to rush more of this technology into hospitals and it honestly terrifies me. Nobody is stopping to ask if this stuff actually works in the real world. We already lived through this nightmare with electronic medical records—they forced that on doctors way too fast, and the computers beeped so much that the doctors just learned to tune them out completely.⏎⏎I keep reading how tech companies test these things at one wealthy hospital where all the data is perfectly clean, and then declare it's ready for the whole country. But real hospital files are a complete mess! You can't just assume a program that works on one specific group of patients is going to work on everyone else. I saw an article about how they make huge mistakes on different populations.⏎⏎And the worst part is we are letting these software companies police themselves. They do their own private certifications. Obviously they are going to say their product is safe, there is too much money involved! We need actual independent proof before this goes into hospitals. When I was teaching, if someone wanted to try a new medication or program on the kids, we demanded actual proof it was safe first. We didn't just take the salesman's word for it. It shouldn't be any different just because it's computers and adults.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Anderson♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔National Minority Quality Forum♔♔♔♔♔♔The National Minority Quality Forum urges HHS to treat equity as the floor for AI adoption, not an afterthought, because communities already harmed by biased tools cannot afford to wait.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Anderson♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Carol went through breast cancer treatment two years back, and I'll be honest, the AI-assisted imaging review they used genuinely impressed me. Dr. Patel at our clinic in Raleigh made the final call, and that mattered. So I'm not against this technology. I really am not. But here is what I keep coming back to, and nobody seems to want to answer it DIRECTLY: the hospitals near where we live, the smaller rural ones, they are CLOSING. Two in our county in the last five years. So when HHS talks about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, I have to ask -- accelerating it WHERE exactly?? Because the way I see it, if you are wealthy or live near a major medical center, AI is going to mean better diagnostics, faster results, a doctor who has more time because the computer handled the routine stuff. But if you live 45 minutes from the nearest hospital and that hospital is already struggling to keep its lights on, what does AI adoption even mean for you? A chatbot? A remote screening tool with nobody to follow up? That does not feel like the same thing. I genuinely do not understand why this request for information does not spend more time asking WHY rural hospitals keep closing before we start talking about layering new technology on top of a system that is already leaving people behind in places like Robeson County.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Walker♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔American College of Rheumatology♔♔♔♔♔♔The American College of Rheumatology (ACR) submits these comments regarding the HHS RFI on accelerating clinical AI adoption. The current implementation sequence is deeply concerning to our membership. Rheumatologists do see value in using algorithms for radiographic scoring of joint damage. However, the push to accelerate this technology ignores reality on the ground.⏎⏎In safety-net settings, the infrastructure to disclose AI use to patients simply is not there. ACR members report that high-Medicaid clinics are deploying diagnostic tools for lupus and rheumatoid arthritis pattern recognition without any protocols to inform patients. This is a massive problem. Non-English-speaking patients navigating complex autoimmune diseases are almost never told how algorithms influence their care. These same patients have the fewest resources to catch or appeal algorithmic errors.⏎⏎Pushing deployment forward without requiring equity impact assessments will widen the outcomes gap and implicates Section 1557 non-discrimination mandates (see 45 CFR Part 92). We are looking at a divided standard of care where well-funded clinics use AI to support physicians, but safety-net facilities use it as a cheap substitute for face-to-face specialist time. The agency must tie any AI acceleration initiatives directly to strict informed consent rules. Independent clinical oversight has to be verified before these tools go live in vulnerable settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Miller♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Donna spent most of 2022 going back and forth to doctors in Lexington before anyone figured out what was happening with her thyroid and I kept thinking the whole time that if they had just caught it sooner she wouldn't have lost so much ground and now I read about these AI systems coming into hospitals and part of me thinks okay yes faster is better but the other part of me keeps circling back to the same worry which is that these machines learned what they know from old medical records and those old records already had the mistakes in them already had the patterns where certain kinds of patients got taken less seriously or got sent home when they should have stayed and I heard something on the news about how there were pulse oximeters reading wrong for darker skin patients for years before anyone said anything and I don't know all the details but the point is the machine doesn't know what it got wrong it just keeps going. So what worries me isn't AI itself I can see why a doctor would want the help what worries me is that HHS seems to want to move fast and speed doesn't fix bias it just spreads it to more people quicker. Donna isn't a data point she's a person and before you put this in every hospital please make sure someone has actually checked whether it treats every kind of patient the same.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Adams♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Allscripts Healthcare Solutions♔♔♔♔♔♔Allscripts Healthcare Solutions submits these comments to note that accelerating AI adoption without binding bias auditing standards will embed documented disparities in prior authorization, sepsis detection, and clinical decision support at scale before health systems have any reliable means of identifying or correcting them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Hernandez♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔NAACP♔♔♔♔♔♔June 12, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎The NAACP urges HHS to reframe this RFI around worker protection and accountability before acceleration.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Kenneth Hernandez⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎NAACP♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Taylor♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I worked as a nurse on cardiac and oncology floors in Denver for over three decades. Over the years I saw plenty of new tech systems pushed on administrators as the next big thing, leaving the bedside staff to deal with the actual mess. Reading the recent STAT News and ProPublica reports about the NaviHealth algorithm cutting off Medicare Advantage patients wasn't shocking to me at all. It just proved what nurses have been seeing for a while. These computer models just look at average recovery times and then spit out a date to terminate coverage, usually without a doctor even looking at it first. So you end up with a patient who can't even get to the bathroom on their own being pushed out the door to go home just because a computer said their time was up.⏎⏎⏎⏎We shouldn't be rushing to put AI into healthcare without figuring out how to protect patients first. A friend of mine named Denise works out at a clinic in Aurora. We were talking recently and she mentioned how treatment recommendations are starting to just show up automatically in patient charts. The doctors don't even know what data the computer used to come up with it. That just sounds like a massive malpractice suit waiting to happen. Denise sees a lot of older folks with diabetes, and she told me about this one woman in her late seventies who barely speaks English. If that woman gets a coverage denial letter in the mail, there is no way she is going to understand it or figure out how to fight it through some complicated appeals process.⏎⏎⏎⏎The whole appeals system is already incredibly frustrating for patients, and honestly it seems like insurance companies make it hard on purpose. They send these thick letters full of jargon with a strict 30-day deadline and no clear instructions on what medical records are actually needed to overturn the decision. Most people just give up. They don't get the rehab or the medical equipment or the infusions they need. The insurance company saves money and their data looks fine because the patient just fell off the radar. Meanwhile that person is at home getting sicker, and nobody is tracking that part of it. This hits certain groups really hard. We are talking about folks with dementia or mobility issues, people on Medicaid who don't have family to advocate for them, and patients living out in rural parts of Colorado who can't easily get to a legal aid office. They get run over by this stuff.⏎⏎⏎⏎The government needs to step in and require a real, licensed clinician to review any AI coverage decision before it ever gets sent to a patient. Patients and their doctors also need to know exactly how these programs make their decisions and where they tend to make mistakes, all written out in plain English. Just telling someone "the algorithm decided" isn't an acceptable medical explanation. If someone gets denied rehab after a hip replacement, they have a right to know exactly what clinical information caused that denial and what they need to provide to fix it. Right now the companies just hide behind proprietary software claims. Appeals need to be easier to access, and there needs to be extra time given to people who have disabilities or language barriers. You can't just let insurance companies exhaust sick people until they quit. We keep seeing things being done just because they are cheaper or easier for the institutions, but we need actual protections in place to keep vulnerable people from falling through the cracks of these new computer systems.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Flores♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎My mother is seventy-three years old and grows zinnias in her backyard in Alabama. She is alive partly because a radiologist in Tuscaloosa spent forty extra minutes with her films when the automated screening found nothing. I think about that a lot when I read federal announcements about accelerating AI deployment in clinical settings, because what I keep coming back to is a question nobody seems to want to answer directly: when the algorithm makes the error that a radiologist would have caught, who is responsible?⏎⏎That is the question this comment is about. I am a librarian, not a doctor or a lawyer, but I have spent considerable time reading about this problem and I believe HHS is moving in a direction that will harm patients unless the accountability structure is fixed first.⏎⏎The current framework, as I understand it, has a basic problem. When AI-assisted clinical decisions cause harm, responsibility can dissolve across a chain of actors. The clinician who followed the AI recommendation, the hospital that deployed the tool, the vendor who built it, the organization whose data trained it. W. Nicholson Price II and colleagues wrote about this in JAMA in 2019, arguing that existing malpractice doctrine does not cleanly assign fault in these situations. That was six years ago and the problem has not been resolved. The number of FDA-cleared AI medical devices has grown considerably since then, and FDA clearance, I have learned, does not mean a tool is safe or effective in any particular hospital with any particular patient population. It means the device met certain pre-market criteria. That is a real distinction and most patients do not know it exists.⏎⏎What makes this worse is that vendor contracts routinely disclaim liability for clinical outcomes. I have read several of these documents in the course of researching this comment, and the pattern is consistent. The vendor says the tool is not intended to replace clinical judgment. The clinician defers to the output. The patient is harmed. Nobody is clearly at fault under existing law.⏎⏎My specific recommendations to HHS are:⏎⏎1. Do not accelerate adoption timelines until minimum liability standards are established for AI-assisted clinical decisions. Reimbursement incentives should not precede accountability frameworks.⏎⏎2. Commission or formally require research into how tort law applies to AI harm chains before expanding FDA clearance pathways. This research should be a precondition for the next phase of adoption policy, not something addressed after the infrastructure is built.⏎⏎3. Require that any AI tool deployed in a clinical setting carry plain-language disclosure to patients about what the tool does, who bears responsibility if it errs, and what recourse exists.⏎⏎4. Examine whether vendor liability waivers in hospital contracts are consistent with existing consumer protection obligations under federal law.⏎⏎I want to be clear that I am not opposed to AI in medicine. If a better screening tool had existed in Tuscaloosa years ago, maybe the radiologist wouldn't have needed to spend that extra forty minutes. I understand the potential. What I am asking is that the speed of deployment not outrun the legal infrastructure that patients depend on when something goes wrong.⏎⏎Appendix A, referenced herein, contains excerpts from vendor contract terms reviewed during preparation of this comment. It is not attached to this submission but is available upon request.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Lee♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Priya had a 104 fever for three days. The whole time, the symptom checker app on our pediatrician's patient portal just kept telling me to "monitor at home" and wouldn't let me schedule an urgent appointment. When I finally just drove her to the actual office and demanded to be seen, the ear infection had already gotten into her mastoid bone. She had to be admitted to the hospital. I'm writing this comment because I'm still so angry about it and I don't want another family going through this.⏎Reading your proposal, I'm trying to figure out whose fault it is when the computer gets it wrong, but you don't really say. After Priya got discharged, I spent weeks trying to get answers. Her doctor basically told me the triage app flagged her as low risk so the nurses didn't override it. But when I complained to the health system, they said the app is from a third-party vendor. I actually looked up the terms of service for that tech company and it literally says the software shouldn't replace medical advice and they aren't liable. The clinic acts like it's out of their hands because of the algorithm. Then the tech company hides behind their fine print. Meanwhile I'm the one left with a traumatized kid and a huge hospital bill. Nobody is actually taking responsibility. Why is the government trying to rush more AI into healthcare when we can't even figure out who to sue when the current stuff fails?⏎Another thing is this whole FDA cleared label. I always thought if the FDA allowed something, that meant it was completely safe. But sitting in the hospital I was reading up on this on my phone. It turns out "cleared" doesn't mean they tested the app on actual sick kids. It mostly just means it's vaguely similar to some older software. But because it has that official FDA stamp, clinics just buy it blindly. If HHS makes it even easier to approve these algorithms, more untested junk is going to end up diagnosing our kids.⏎Also, I asked the clinic manager why they don't just delete the app since it obviously missed a severe infection. She told me it's hardwired into their entire electronic records and billing system now. They spent millions setting it up and they're basically stuck with it. So even when the software makes dangerous mistakes, the patients are the ones who suffer because it's too expensive for the hospital to switch to a different program.⏎I get that computers are everywhere now. I'm 37, I'm not a luddite. But you can't just unleash all this new AI without making sure someone is on the hook when things go wrong. Right now it's just a giant loophole for tech companies to get rich. Before you speed up AI adoption, you need to figure out how a parent is supposed to get justice if an app hurts their child. If the software gives dangerous medical advice, who legally has to pay for it? And how do we force a clinic to stop using an algorithm that is proven to be unsafe? I still haven't gotten straight answers from anyone involved in Priya's care. Honestly, I'm still trying to fight our insurance over the hospital stay that wouldn't have even happened if a real human had just answered the phone in the first place.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Miller♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔UCSF Health♔♔♔♔♔♔UCSF HEALTH RESPONSE TO HHS HEALTH SECTOR AI REQUEST FOR INFORMATION⏎⏎⏎⏎UCSF Health submits these comments based on our direct experience as an academic medical center navigating inconsistent AI deployment standards. A recent internal policy review across our clinical departments and affiliated care sites found that dozens of distinct reimbursement classification frameworks were applied to non-device AI tools in a single year. Navigating this fragmented regulatory environment requires significant administrative resources, which our government relations team documents on an ongoing basis.⏎⏎⏎⏎Regarding the agency's questions on accelerating adoption, HHS appears to be encouraging faster implementation before the industry has established reliable methods to verify equitable performance across diverse patient populations. We have already seen the clinical consequences of unexamined bias in medical technology, ranging from the well-documented performance discrepancies of pulse oximeters in Black patients to the disparities identified in prior authorization algorithms affecting Latino patients. Moving forward with large-scale AI deployment in the absence of standardized bias auditing frameworks risks compounding these historical inequities. We strongly recommend that HHS prioritize funding for bias auditing research before taking further steps to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Rodriguez♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔Alliance for Health Policy♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Alliance for Health Policy, we urge HHS and ONC to slow deployment timelines and require that patient-centered relationship standards govern any AI tool before clinical adoption proceeds.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Clark♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔Medical Device Manufacturers Association♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS is repeating the EHR consolidation disaster in real time, and MS patients who already distrust AI-driven care tools will pay the price when vendor lock-in makes course correction impossible.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Hill♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty years on med-surg and cardiac floors in Ohio, and the thing I keep thinking about is my neighbor Carla. She's in her sixties, diabetic, and about two years ago she went into Riverside Methodist with what a software system flagged as non-cardiac. The flag almost got her discharged early. Her nurse pushed back, they did the cath, and she had a real MI with atypical presentation. If that nurse had deferred to the screen instead of the patient in front of her, Carla is dead. That's not a hypothetical.⏎⏎What worries me about HHS accelerating AI adoption right now is who gets left without that nurse in the room. I've watched long enough to know how this plays out:⏎⏎1. Patients with resources, good insurance, English as a first language, get AI as one more tool alongside attentive human care.⏎2. Patients who are poor, elderly, disabled, or don't speak English get AI instead of that care, because staffing costs money and the algorithm is cheaper.⏎⏎HHS is supposed to protect everyone equally, that's not optional. Before anything gets accelerated, I want to see mandatory equity impact assessments run independently, not by the vendors selling the systems. I genuinely don't know why that isn't already the starting point before any of this moves forward.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Davis♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My Medicare card. I applied in September and it is now December and I have called four times and no one can tell me where it is. I understand this is an RFI about artificial intelligence in hospitals, but HHS runs Medicare too, and if the department cannot mail me a card in three months, I genuinely do not understand why anyone thinks it can oversee the responsible rollout of AI making clinical decisions about cancer diagnoses. I spent thirty years on the oncology floor at Brigham and I watched what happens when administrators trust systems over people.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Perez♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Alliance for Aging Research♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Alliance for Aging Research, we want to raise something this RFI consistently sidesteps: the word "accelerating" in the title is doing a lot of work, and not in a good way. Our member survey data, drawn from 287 radiology practices in 2024, found that 43% had no formal protocol for auditing AI tools for differential performance across patient demographic groups. That is the current baseline. Pressing harder on adoption without addressing that gap first is not progress for older patients.⏎⏎We keep returning to the dental coverage situation as an analogy. Seniors have been told for years that meaningful coverage improvements are coming, and the policy machinery keeps moving without delivering the thing that actually matters to the people affected. The AI conversation has the same shape to it. The announcements are frequent. The infrastructure for accountability is not there.⏎⏎HHS should commission an independent structural market analysis before issuing any acceleration-oriented guidance. Vendors with active HHS partnerships should be explicitly excluded from participating in the standards development process for AI oversight frameworks.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Robinson♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔University of South Carolina Arnold School of Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎The University of South Carolina Arnold School of Public Health respectfully submits these comments. Bias auditing across demographic subgroups must precede any reimbursement incentive, not follow it.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Donna Robinson⏎Public Health Researcher⏎University of South Carolina Arnold School of Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Young♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎My name is Margaret Young. I am a licensed clinical social worker based in Atlanta, Georgia, and I have worked in hospital-adjacent case management for over fifteen years. I am submitting this comment in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, and I want to be direct about where I am coming from: I have a client, Darius, a 34-year-old Black man, who waited six hours in a hospital triage queue during an active suicidal crisis because an algorithm classified him as low-acuity. His attending nurse told me afterward she believed his zip code and insurance status were embedded variables in the predictive model. He survived. That is not a given.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on two of the questions this RFI raises: what safeguards should accompany accelerated AI deployment, and how HHS should address equity concerns in clinical AI.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI treats AI adoption primarily as a question of speed. I understand why. There are real access problems in American healthcare, and AI tools can help close gaps in diagnostic capacity, especially in rural and underserved settings. I am not dismissing that. But the "democratizing access" framing worries me because it tends to obscure a predictable pattern: higher-income patients receive AI as a supplement to human clinical judgment, while lower-income patients receive AI as a substitute for it. That is not a market outcome HHS should be neutral about. It is a policy failure, and it is one HHS has legal obligations to prevent under Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act, which prohibits discrimination in health programs receiving federal financial assistance.⏎⏎HHS's Office for Civil Rights has issued guidance making clear that nondiscrimination requirements apply to the full range of program activities, including the tools and systems used to make clinical decisions. An algorithm that systematically deprioritizes patients based on socioeconomic proxies is not a neutral instrument. Accelerating its deployment without requiring equity impact assessments first is, in my reading of that guidance, an affirmative compliance problem.⏎⏎I want to say plainly what I think the minimum requirements should be before any acceleration of deployment:⏎⏎1. Mandatory equity impact assessments, conducted before deployment, with results publicly disclosed, for any AI tool used in triage, acuity scoring, or clinical prioritization.⏎⏎2. Mandatory human review thresholds for any AI recommendation that affects care timing for patients in behavioral health crisis, regardless of the efficiency cost.⏎⏎3. A clear process by which patients, or their advocates (including social workers), can flag a concern about an AI-generated classification and receive a human response within a defined timeframe.⏎⏎Vulnerable populations, including elderly patients, patients with disabilities, non-English speakers, and patients experiencing mental health crises, are the least equipped to identify when an algorithm has made an error about them, and the least likely to have a physician who will catch it quickly. The risk of AI failure is not distributed evenly. It concentrates where oversight is thinnest.⏎⏎I genuinely do not understand how HHS can square an acceleration agenda with its own stated equity commitments unless equity impact assessment is built into the deployment process as a precondition, not an afterthought.⏎⏎A note: I have referenced supporting documentation in an appendix that is not attached to this submission due to file size limitations. I am prepared to provide it upon request.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Gonzalez♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔Navina♔♔♔♔♔♔Navina Compliance Review < > HHS AI RFI Response < > Page 1⏎navina.ai < > compliance@navina.ai⏎⏎The compliance review our organization completed across fourteen deployed AI-assisted diagnostic tools — encompassing feedback from forty-seven health system integration partners — found that the question of deployment equity was not raised as a formal consideration in a single implementation agreement reviewed during the 2023–2024 cycle. Not one. That finding is what compels this submission.⏎⏎HHS's framing of this RFI treats acceleration as an unambiguous policy good. What I observe across our integration partner network does not support that framing. The deployment pattern we see consistently is this: well-resourced health systems are deploying AI as a supplement to clinical staffing, with robust override protocols and physician review loops. Under-resourced systems — safety-net hospitals, rural FQHCs, Medicaid-dominant practices — are deploying the same or comparable tools as a staffing substitute, because that is the economic rationale that makes implementation financially viable for them. The National Academy for State Health Policy and Georgetown University Health Policy Institute documented this substitution risk in their 2024 analysis of AI deployment patterns in Medicaid settings, and what they described as a theoretical concern is, in our compliance experience, already operational.⏎⏎Navina Comments < > HHS AI RFI Response < > Page 2⏎⏎This matters for HHS specifically because of what it implies for the populations receiving care in those under-resourced settings. Patients with complex, multi-morbid, or atypical presentations are statistically overrepresented among elderly, low-income, and non-English-speaking populations. These are also the populations where current AI clinical decision-support tools are most likely to produce erroneous outputs — not because the tools are poorly designed in isolation, but because they were validated on datasets that do not adequately represent these presentations. Obermeyer et al. documented in Science (2019) how a widely deployed population health algorithm systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients, and the mechanism was not malicious intent but training data that proxied cost for need. The liability exposure for AI developers and deployers when that failure mode intersects with a protected class is not hypothetical.⏎⏎HHS has existing legal obligations here that this RFI does not adequately acknowledge. Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act and its implementing regulations at 45 C.F.R. Part 92 prohibit discrimination in health programs receiving federal financial assistance. The Office for Civil Rights issued guidance in 2023 specifically addressing nondiscrimination obligations in programs using automated decision-making systems. Accelerating AI adoption without a mandatory equity impact assessment requirement as a precondition for any reimbursement pathway or regulatory accommodation is not a neutral facilitation of innovation — it is an affirmative regulatory choice to defer those civil rights obligations to a later date that may never arrive.⏎⏎The concrete policy ask is straightforward: HHS should condition any new reimbursement pathway or regulatory accommodation for AI clinical tools on the submission and public disclosure of a pre-deployment equity impact assessment, with specific attention to performance stratified by race, language preference, disability status, and payer mix. The methodology for those assessments needs to be standardized by HHS before the reimbursement pathways open, not after. What the assessment template should require for safety-net deployment contexts specifically is something we would⏎⏎Navina Comments < > HHS AI RFI Response < > Page 3⏎⏎welcome the opportunity to address in a supplemental submission, if HHS opens a technical comment period on assessment design.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Johnson♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔National Health Council♔♔♔♔♔♔National Health Council Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The National Health Council urges HHS to reject accelerationist framing in this RFI and require meaningful human-centered safeguards before any further expansion of AI in clinical care settings.⏎⏎National Health Council Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Garcia♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-seven years at the bedside taught me that Dolores, my Crow patient in Billings, needed someone who could see her go quiet when she didn't trust you. No algorithm reads that silence. Please slow down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Ramirez♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My patient Maria, a woman in her sixties on the cardiac unit at University Hospital in Newark, waited three extra days for the right diagnosis because the system kept flagging her symptoms as atypical. We found out later the reference data skewed heavily male. That wasn't an AI system, technically, but it was the same problem: BAD DATA baked into clinical tools, and nobody could easily pull it out once it was embedded. That's what I keep coming back to here. HHS is being asked to ACCELERATE adoption before the auditing frameworks even exist, and I genuinely don't understand why that's considered acceptable. Once AI is woven into reimbursement structures and clinical workflows, it becomes a one-way door, same as every EHR rollout I watched happen and nobody could reverse. This is a values question, not just a technical one, and the public hasn't actually gotten to answer it yet.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Harris♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔National Federation of the Blind♔♔♔♔♔♔National Federation of the Blind | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎⏎The National Federation of the Blind submits this comment with concern about the framing of this RFI. Our 2023 review of 47 dental practices serving federally qualified health center populations found that 62% lacked standardized AI disclosure protocols, with non-English-speaking and Medicaid-enrolled patients least likely to receive any explanation at all.⏎⏎National Federation of the Blind | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The question of how much AI belongs in medicine is a values question, not a technical one. HHS should not use reimbursement structures and research levers simultaneously to foreclose that deliberation. Once AI is embedded in clinical workflows, the political cost of reversing course will always exceed the cost of tolerating known harms. This is the EHR adoption pattern repeating.⏎⏎HHS should establish independent governance prerequisites before accelerating further.⏎⏎nfb-policy@nfb.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔White♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I saw a link on Facebook about the government taking comments on AI in hospitals and decided to write in. I'm a firefighter here in Mississippi, not a computer person, but I've been worrying about this a lot lately. My husband Marcus has a heart thing that we didn't know about until a physical a couple years ago. The nurse doing his EKG saw something weird on the readout and actually went and got the doctor instead of just trusting the printout. I honestly think about that all the time with all this AI stuff in the news. If they were just relying on a computer program that day, would they have caught it?⏎⏎It scares me that doctors are so busy they might just start clicking "agree" on whatever the computer screen says. If a machine says you're fine, a tired doctor is probably going to just move on to the next room. And computers mess up. I saw on the news a while back about some hospital computer system that was supposed to figure out who needed extra care, but it was basically skipping over Black patients. People used it for ages before anybody even realized it was messed up. It just shows that these programs aren't perfect and you can't just assume they know what they're doing.⏎⏎The document I read said something about how these AI companies would certify their own programs. It completely baffles me how outside verification wasn't the absolute bare minimum requirement from the jump. At the firehouse, our breathing apparatus and turnout gear gets tested by outside safety groups. We don't just take the manufacturer's word for it that the tank won't fail in a fire, because the company making it isn't the one running into the burning building. It should be the exact same for medical software. You need some kind of totally separate group testing this stuff, not the tech guys who are just trying to sell it to hospitals to make a profit.⏎⏎And testing it once in a lab somewhere isn't enough anyway. You have to keep checking it after the hospitals buy it. A lot of the hospitals around here are struggling. We don't have the fancy equipment they have at big city research hospitals, and our local clinics are always short-staffed. If you test an AI program at some massive rich hospital, that doesn't mean it's going to work the same way at a small county hospital down here. It has to be proven to work for everybody, and somebody needs to be constantly watching it to make sure it's actually helping people and not just saving time for the hospital administrators.⏎⏎Like I said I don't know the first thing about writing laws. I just know that safety means actually having a human being paying attention. Please don't let tech companies push this through without real rules.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Johnson♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Back in 2022, a regional hospital in Kearney, Nebraska, misdiagnosed my mother, and she was handed yet another incorrect diagnosis twelve months later. The doctor who finally spotted the inconsistency in her scans was a younger physician who ignored the software's recommendations and ordered the follow-up testing himself. I have reflected on that incident often, and my main takeaway remains the same: the technology did not save her. A person did.⏎⏎I say this not to dismiss AI in medicine, but because I think it matters enormously to the framing of what HHS is asking here. The RFI is organized around acceleration. The word appears repeatedly. What concerns me is that the question of what we might be accelerating past is treated as secondary, if it is treated at all.⏎⏎There is a body of research, including work by Kelley and colleagues, showing that the quality of the patient-physician relationship independently predicts adherence, recovery, and patient satisfaction, separate from the technical quality of care delivered. That finding has been replicated enough times that it should function as a constraint on policy, not a footnote to it. The relationship is part of the treatment. That is not a soft claim or a sentimental one. It is a clinical finding.⏎⏎What I observe, both from my own experience and from conversations with people in my community, is that clinical encounters are already moving in a direction where the physician is managing a dashboard as much as seeing a patient. I have sat in exam rooms where the clinician's eyes were on a screen for most of the visit. I don't say that as a complaint about any individual doctor. I say it because the structural incentives are already pushing that way, and deploying more AI tools without strong human-centered requirements will accelerate that drift.⏎⏎My mother's health records now exist in at least four separate systems across Nebraska and one in Colorado. I have no meaningful way to know who is drawing inferences from that data, whether those inferences are accurate for a woman of her age and background, or whether any of the AI tools touching her records have been validated on populations that look like her. These are not hypothetical concerns.⏎⏎If HHS moves forward with actions to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, I would ask the Department to require, at minimum:⏎⏎1. That any AI tool used in direct clinical decision support be validated on demographically representative populations before deployment, with public documentation of where gaps exist.⏎2. That patients be notified in plain language when AI has contributed to a diagnosis or treatment recommendation they receive.⏎3. That reimbursement structures not create financial incentives for reducing the time a clinician spends in direct, unmediated conversation with a patient.⏎⏎The question I keep returning to is not whether AI can improve clinical care in some circumstances. I think it probably can. The question is whether the Department is prepared to require that acceleration not come at the cost of the human relationship that the research consistently shows matters to outcomes.⏎⏎My mother's younger physician did not have better software than his colleagues. He had more time, or took more time, to look carefully. I'm not sure how you write a regulation that protects that, but I think that is the harder and more important problem.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Miller♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔Carestream Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Carestream Health | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Christopher Miller, Healthcare Administrator, Carestream Health, Washington State⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY OBSERVATIONS⏎⏎The framing of this RFI concerns me in ways I want to be precise about. Acceleration, as an organizing principle for AI deployment in clinical settings, assumes that the primary obstacle is regulatory friction. That assumption is incorrect. The primary obstacle is that no coherent liability chain exists for AI-assisted clinical decision support tools once they leave the development environment and enter real operational contexts. Sancian LLC's 2024 internal compliance analysis across 47 health technology clients found that 68% encountered inconsistent coverage determination criteria for AI-assisted CDS tools falling outside FDA device classification thresholds, and that indemnification language addressing AI-generated recommendations was absent or insufficiently defined in 81% of payer agreements reviewed. That is not a friction problem. That is a structural accountability failure, and accelerating deployment into that gap does not reduce harm exposure — it multiplies it.⏎⏎⏎THE LIABILITY CHAIN IS BROKEN⏎⏎Price, Gerke, and Cohen noted in their 2019 JAMA analysis that AI deployment in clinical settings creates a diffuse liability environment in which no clean tort assignment exists between the clinician, the vendor, and the hospital. That observation has not been resolved in the years since. If anything, the contractual landscape has worsened. Sancian's compliance cost modeling estimated that meeting divergent payer evaluation requirements added an average of $340,000 per product per market entry cycle, which creates a perverse dynamic: only well-capitalized vendors survive market entry, and those vendors are precisely the ones with sufficient legal resources to draft disclaimer language that insulates them from downstream liability.⏎⏎⏎Carestream Health | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎FDA clearance does not resolve this. The FDA's own 2021 AI/ML-Based SaMD Action Plan acknowledged that device clearance validates pre-deployment performance, not deployment-context safety — meaning a cleared tool can behave within its validated parameters and still generate systematically harmful recommendations in a specific clinical workflow. Mello et al. observed in their 2020 JAMA review that existing tort frameworks were not designed to handle distributed, algorithmic decision-making chains, and I would argue the contractual frameworks are even less prepared.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD ACTUALLY DO⏎⏎Obermeyer and Emanuel identified in their 2016 NEJM paper that workflow dependency dynamics make post-adoption course correction prohibitively costly once AI tools are embedded in clinical operations. Lock-in is not a theoretical risk. It is a documented feature of enterprise health IT deployments, and payer agreements that omit indemnification language at the point of contracting leave that risk entirely with the administrator and the patient.⏎⏎HHS should condition any reimbursement pathway expansion on mandatory pre-deployment liability frameworks and standardized indemnification requirements — not as guidance documents, but as enforceable prerequisites. Optional frameworks do not change behavior; they provide cover for inaction.⏎⏎I have submitted comments to three separate federal RFIs over the past several years and have received no substantive response to any of them. I genuinely do not know whether⏎⏎Carestream Health | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 3⏎⏎carestream.comments@healthadmin.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Green♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔National Psoriasis Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Psoriasis Foundation, we urge HHS to establish clear accountability structures before accelerating AI adoption, as no responsible framework currently assigns liability when algorithmic tools harm patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Gonzalez♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: Re: HHS AI RFI submission -- Emily's comment for the docket⏎⏎My client Maria spent three months getting bounced around by an automated screening tool in Dayton before anyone caught she had stage II cervical cancer. The algorithm hadn't been tested on people like her. I read about what UnitedHealth was doing with the NaviHealth tool -- denying post-acute care claims by the thousands -- and it sounded exactly like what happened to her. Most of my clients never appeal a denial. Not because it was right, but because the process wears you down on purpose. HHS has authority here and I want to know why you're not using it. Fix the appeals process before you accelerate anything.⏎⏎-- Emily Gonzalez, MSW⏎Dayton, OH♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Hill♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔Wayne State University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Wayne State University School of Medicine respectfully requests that HHS condition any AI reimbursement incentives on rigorous external validation requirements and independent post-deployment surveillance before clinical certification proceeds.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Adams♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a nurse here in Montana, I see patients wait months for results and just worry themselves sick. Computers might help speed up getting those answers. But HHS still has to require real human oversight at every decision point. You can't just leave patient care entirely up to a machine.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Adams♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔AI Now Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the AI Now Institute, Matthew Adams submits these comments in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎The RFI frames acceleration as the obvious goal. We are not convinced that is the right frame. What gets lost in efficiency-centered adoption models is something a physician in rural Oklahoma described to our research team in March 2024: the moment a patient decides whether to disclose something true and frightening depends entirely on who is in the room with them.⏎⏎HHS should ask what limits on AI preserve what medicine actually is, not merely how fast adoption can move. The AI Now Institute urges the Department to reframe accordingly before any regulatory or reimbursement actions proceed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Clark♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am submitting these comments regarding the HHS proposal to speed up the use of AI in healthcare. I am a 60-year-old office manager from Texas. I am a big supporter of medical technology because it saved my life. A few months ago, my cardiologist ran my stress test through a new AI program and it spotted a blockage that regular tests missed. I ended up needing a stent right away instead of waiting until I had a heart attack. I am very grateful that software was available.⏎⏎⏎⏎However, going through that experience made me worry about what happens when hospitals rush to use new systems. I have run a busy office for thirty years. Dealing with financial software has taught me that when a computer screen gives an answer, employees usually just accept it without asking if it makes sense. If doctors do that with AI, patients will get hurt. I remember reading how pulse oximeters often give bad readings for Black patients. When you feed bad information into a computer, the results will be wrong. A doctor might question a weird reading from a standard medical device, but they might just assume a high-tech computer program is always right. I also saw a news report about a major software vendor that pushed out a sepsis prediction tool that didn't work as well in real hospitals as the company promised, yet it was already being used everywhere.⏎⏎⏎⏎At my job, I would never let a vendor roll out a new billing system without testing it on our own computers with our own files first. It shouldn't be any different for medicine. Regarding the Department's question on regulations, HHS needs to make sure outside groups test these tools for bias before letting them on any fast track. It needs to be a strict rule from day one, not just a suggestion to check on things down the road.⏎⏎⏎⏎This is incredibly important for the AI that insurance companies use to approve or deny care. We know there are already problems with Black and Latino patients getting their treatments approved. Because AI learns from old insurance records, those past unfair decisions will just get copied by the computer. The software will keep denying care to the same groups of people unless someone is forced to look for those patterns before the system goes live.⏎⏎⏎⏎I want medical AI to succeed because it can clearly do a lot of good. But HHS needs to put safety checks first. We cannot let hospitals and insurance companies rely on these programs until independent testers prove they work correctly for all patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Rodriguez♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Komodo Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Komodo Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO THE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: Health Sector Artificial Intelligence Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY OBSERVATIONS ON MARKET STRUCTURE⏎⏎Komodo Health submits these comments with direct experience in health data infrastructure and AI-enabled analytics across large integrated delivery networks. Our governance work has required sustained engagement with the vendor contracting dynamics this RFI largely takes for granted, and what we observe is not an industry awaiting regulatory permission to grow — it is an industry actively consolidating around proprietary data architectures before adequate structural safeguards exist.⏎⏎The RFI's framing positions HHS as an accelerant. That framing concerns us, because the Department has been in this position before.⏎⏎⏎THE EHR ANALOGY DESERVES SERIOUS ATTENTION⏎⏎The HITECH Act drove substantial hospital EHR adoption — Adler-Milstein and Jha documented this in Health Affairs (2017, 36:8) — but the market competition that was supposed to follow ONC certification never materialized in any meaningful way. A small number of vendors captured dominant market positions, and proprietary data formats became the mechanism. ONC's own 2023 report on health IT market concentration documented how interoperability barriers persist specifically because they are commercially rational for incumbent vendors to maintain. That is not a coincidence. It is a structural feature.⏎⏎AI vendor markets are replicating this pattern, and in some respects accelerating it. EHR integration dependencies mean that AI tools deployed at the point of care are, in practice, evaluated partly on their compatibility with existing EHR infrastructure. This creates access asymmetries that favor large vendors with established integration agreements. Dafny, Gruber, and Ody (NBER Working Paper No. 29771, 2022) documented how consolidation in health care markets generates durable competitive harms that are difficult to reverse after the fact.⏎⏎Komodo Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE PUBLIC-PRIVATE PARTNERSHIP PROBLEM⏎⏎Public-private AI research partnerships carry a regulatory capture risk that the RFI does not acknowledge. When large technology vendors gain sustained policy access through research collaborations with HHS agencies, smaller competitors and public-interest researchers do not benefit equivalently. The FTC and DOJ's 2023 Merger Guidelines flagged data-driven market considerations in digital platform contexts precisely because data access asymmetries compound over time in ways that standard antitrust review misses.⏎⏎HHS should condition any AI acceleration initiative on three structural requirements: mandatory interoperability standards for AI outputs analogous to USCDI data classes, open data format requirements that prohibit proprietary lock-in as a contractual mechanism, and conflict-of-interest firewalls governing vendor participation in federally funded AI evaluation programs. These are not novel policy instruments — they are lessons the Department was supposed to learn from ONC certification history and, based on this RFI, does not appear to have fully internalized.⏎⏎The window for imposing these structural conditions before market consolidation forecloses the option is, in our assessment,♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Davis♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My buddy Marcus died eight months after he got home from his third deployment because an automated screening tool missed what a doctor sitting across from him might have caught. I get that HHS wants to MOVE FAST on AI adoption but before you accelerate anything, somebody needs to answer one question: WHO BEARS THE RISK?? Veterans on the VA system, elderly patients, people on Medicaid, rural patients who already wait six months for an appointment — these are the people getting the automated version. Wealthy patients get human-plus-AI. That's not democratizing access, that's a two-tier system with better branding. And where exactly were DRUG COMPANIES when we needed transparency rules?? Make them publish their R&D spending before you add one more reporting burden on any hospital trying to actually care for people.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔King♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I'm writing this because of an appointment at my cardiologist in Baton Rouge a few months ago. I was getting chest tightness. I thought it was just dehydration from pulling rebar all summer, but they hooked me up to some new AI screening tool and it found something the normal EKG didn't see. I got a referral. I'm glad it caught it, don't get me wrong. But the whole thing didn't sit right with me.⏎⏎⏎⏎I asked the doctor how the computer saw something she couldn't, and nobody could give me a straight answer. They just looked at the screen and trusted it. I've been working construction down here in Louisiana for decades. Being a black man pushing 60, I know the medical system hasn't always treated folks in my community right. Did the computer program know my background? Did it factor in my race or the physical toll my job takes? They couldn't tell me. It made me feel like just another data point.⏎⏎⏎⏎If HHS lets clinics put this AI stuff everywhere, you need to look at what it does to doctor visits. I wanted the doctor to actually look at me and ask about my work, the heat, the stress. A computer program doesn't ask about any of that, it just reads whatever numbers get typed in. I'm worried hospitals will just use this to rush people out the door faster to save a buck, and that ruins the trust you need to actually follow a doctor's advice.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS has to put some guardrails on this. You can't just have tech companies running healthcare. There always needs to be an actual doctor putting their name on the line. If the computer is wrong, the doctor has to be responsible. We also have a right to know how it's making decisions. If it's using my race or zip code to decide my care, explain that to me in plain English. You also need to make sure these programs actually work on everyday working people in the south. Testing it on people at a fancy college hospital doesn't mean it works for my crew in Baton Rouge.⏎⏎⏎⏎It's hard enough getting folks in my neighborhood to go to the doctor because of bad history with hospitals. If you bring AI in without showing who takes the blame when it screws up, people are going to trust the system even less. If a program misses a blocked artery and someone gets hurt, who gets sued? Nobody seems to know. Please fix that before paying for all this new tech.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Robinson♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mama went eight months with ovarian cancer that nobody caught, and I still think about how different things might have been if somebody had been paying closer attention. The clinics she went to were using billing software that mixed up her records twice. Nobody got fired for that. Nobody even seemed all that concerned. So when I hear HHS talking about accelerating AI adoption in hospitals and clinics, the first thing I think about is not the technology. It's who made the decision to buy it, and why.⏎⏎I run a small business in Baton Rouge and I know how business decisions get made. When a health system buys an AI tool, they are not thinking about my mama. They are thinking about reducing nursing staff, speeding up workflows, and getting more patients through the door. The people who are going to get replaced or watched by productivity software are the same nurses and billing clerks who caught the things the computers missed for her. A lot of those workers are women, a lot of them are Black, and their judgment is exactly what kept my mama's case from getting worse faster than it already did. I genuinely do not understand why HHS would accelerate any of this before figuring out how to make sure those workers stay in the room. Slow down and put patients and frontline staff first before whoever is selling the software gets their contracts signed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Martinez♔♔New York♔♔United States♔Disability Rights Advocates♔♔♔♔♔♔Disability Rights Advocates urges HHS to impose structural interoperability conditions and independent oversight requirements before any AI vendor consolidation in clinical care becomes irreversible.⏎⏎My former patient Maria—a woman in her sixties I cared for during my clinical tenure on a cardiac unit in Newark—was misdiagnosed twice because the documentation system persistently flagged her symptoms as "atypical," a flaw we later realized occurred partly because the reference data skewed heavily male. HHS is being asked to ACCELERATE adoption before the auditing frameworks even exist, and it is completely baffling that this approach is deemed acceptable. This missing accountability is the primary issue our organization must emphasize.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Wilson♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔Caption Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Caption Health | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: Health Sector Artificial Intelligence Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY OBSERVATION⏎⏎Caption Health supports modernized reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted diagnostic tools. That said, the RFI's emphasis on acceleration raises a governance question that the Department should address before moving forward on any of the reimbursement or regulatory relief mechanisms under consideration.⏎⏎⏎THE BIAS DOCUMENTATION PROBLEM⏎⏎The clinical record on algorithmic bias is not ambiguous at this point. Obermeyer et al. (Science, 2019) demonstrated that a widely deployed population health algorithm systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients, producing measurable disparities in care allocation. Wong et al. (JAMA Internal Medicine, 2021) found that a proprietary sepsis prediction model performed materially worse on external validation than its developers had reported, with differential accuracy across patient subgroups. Sjoding et al. (NEJM, 2020) documented that pulse oximeters — a hardware tool, not software — showed statistically significant measurement error in patients with darker skin pigmentation, a finding that went undetected for decades because the validation datasets were not demographically disaggregated. The pattern across these studies is consistent: tools that appear validated in aggregate can cause systematic harm at the subgroup level when disaggregated performance data is not required at the point of market entry.⏎⏎Caption Health | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT STRUCTURE CREATES PERVERSE INCENTIVES⏎⏎Our organization's experience reviewing payer contracts has revealed that 83% of agreements executed between 2022 and 2024 contained materially inconsistent definitions distinguishing clinical decision support from medical device software, which produces divergent liability treatment for functionally identical AI-driven tools. This inconsistency, combined with reimbursement denial rates that our analysis found to be approximately 34% higher for AI-assisted interpretive tools than for traditional diagnostic codes, means that mid-tier developers face strong financial pressure to minimize pre-deployment validation costs. Gianfrancesco et al. (JAMA Internal Medicine, 2018) observed that EHR-derived training data carries systematic biases related to healthcare access patterns, which means that compressed validation timelines are particularly likely to miss demographic performance gaps. The HHS OIG's 2022 report on Medicare Advantage prior authorization denials (OEI-09-18-00260) is relevant context here: when financial incentives and oversight gaps align, documented patient harm follows.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE⏎⏎The Department should condition any reimbursement pathway modernization on mandatory pre-deployment bias auditing with demographic disaggregation requirements — specifically, stratified performance reporting by race, sex, age, and socioeconomic proxy variables, submitted to CMS prior to any new AI-assisted diagnostic code becoming billable. This is not a novel concept; FDA's action plan for AI/ML-based SaMD already references performance monitoring across subpopulations, and HHS could build directly on that framework rather than starting from scratch.⏎⏎One practical concern our compliance team has raised is what the audit submission format would look like and who reviews it, because right now there is no⏎⏎Caption Health | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎⏎designated clearinghouse for that kind of demographic performance data at CMS, and without one, the requirement becomes effectively unenforceable regardless of how well-intentioned the underlying policy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Young♔♔California♔♔United States♔Exscientia♔♔♔♔♔♔UpDoc represents 214 health AI developer and health technology organizations, and we submit these comments in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. Our 2024 member survey found that 67% of respondents identified payer-to-payer inconsistency in coverage and coding as their single greatest barrier to scaling clinically validated tools — a finding that shapes everything we say below.⏎⏎The framing of "acceleration" deserves scrutiny before HHS acts on it. Acceleration is not inherently beneficial if the infrastructure required to sustain safe deployment has not been built. We have seen this pattern before. The nationwide EHR rollout of the 2010s proceeded on an optimistic timeline that outpaced the workflow integration work required to make those systems safe and usable. The result was documented in ONC Data Brief No. 58 (2022), which catalogued widespread clinical decision support alert fatigue and the workflow disruptions that followed. The lesson was not that EHRs were a bad idea. The lesson was that deployment speed and deployment readiness are not the same thing.⏎⏎The sepsis prediction literature offers a more recent and more pointed cautionary example. Sendak and colleagues (2020, NPJ Digital Medicine) documented that a machine learning sepsis model which performed well under validation conditions showed meaningful degradation when deployed in real clinical environments — a finding that was not detectable through pre-deployment testing alone. This is not an edge case. Dataset shift, the phenomenon by which a model trained on one data distribution encounters systematically different data in production, is a structural feature of health AI deployment, as Finlayson and colleagues documented in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2021.⏎⏎We want to be careful here not to overstate the pessimism. These tools can work. Many of our member organizations have invested significantly in rigorous post-deployment monitoring and have the clinical outcomes data to show for it.⏎⏎The problem is that this investment is not rewarded by current reimbursement or regulatory structures. A developer who conducts continuous real-world performance evaluation competes on price against a developer who does not, with no mechanism for payers or health systems to distinguish between them. That is a structural incentive problem, not a cultural one, and it will not self-correct.⏎⏎There is also the question of automation bias, which Obermeyer and Lee described in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2017: clinicians who rely on AI-assisted decision support tend to reduce their own vigilance in ways that can increase error rates when the tool is performing at its worst. The irony is that a tool which appears to perform well can make clinical errors more likely, not less, particularly in the fragmented, inconsistently coded real-world data environments that differ substantially from the cleaner training sets on which these tools were validated. ECRI's 2023 annual hazard report identified AI-assisted diagnostic tools specifically as a top-ten health technology hazard for precisely this reason.⏎⏎What we are asking HHS to consider is this: condition any accelerated reimbursement pathway or safe harbor on demonstrated real-world performance benchmarks, not just pre-deployment validation. The specific threshold methodology can be developed through notice-and-comment. We recognize that is a significant additional step and we do not minimize the compliance burden it would create for smaller developers.⏎⏎pcomments@hhs-healthai-rfi.gov⏎⏎UpDoc Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎That said, the absence of such a requirement is itself what creates the race-to-the-bottom dynamic that ultimately disadvantages developers who build responsibly — and we have 40 member organizations who said exactly that at our roundtable in September.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Brown♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the HHS,⏎⏎⏎⏎You need to test these medical computer tools for bias before you let hospitals use them on us, not wait until after.⏎⏎⏎⏎I go to the VA over in Spokane. In 2019 my cardiologist Dr. Reyes used a computer program that caught a weird rhythm on my EKG that the human technician totally missed. I got a pacemaker out of it, so I know these machines can help.⏎⏎⏎⏎But I sit in a women's veterans group and half these ladies have had their symptoms brushed off for years. I get real worried about what happens when hospitals pull the humans out just to speed things up. A machine only knows what you feed into it. If it learns from decades of old charts where women's pain was ignored, it will just do the exact same thing. And if a fancy computer spits out a result, doctors will just trust it blindly and nobody catches the mistakes.⏎⏎⏎⏎Audit these systems before they go into clinics. We already fought hard enough to get decent care at the VA, we shouldn't have to argue with an algorithm too.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Miller♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔Enlitic♔♔♔♔♔♔Enlitic⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Response⏎⏎Docket HHS-2024-AI-001⏎⏎Joshua Miller, CFO⏎⏎Enlitic⏎⏎St. Louis, Missouri 63101⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎⏎THE PERFORMANCE GAP IS NOT INCIDENTAL⏎⏎⏎⏎Thirty years of health IT implementation science show a consistent trend where systems performing well in controlled validation degrade substantially when deployed into real-world clinical workflows. We have already seen this happen in practice. When the Epic Sepsis Model was externally validated in 2021, the JAMA Internal Medicine study showed the algorithm failed to identify most sepsis cases while generating thousands of false positives. This type of failure represents a structural reality of how health IT products move from benchmark to deployment, and is not simply an isolated anomaly. Similar performance degradation occurs frequently when machine learning models face patient populations and data environments that diverge from their training context (Shah et al., JAMA). HHS's framing of AI acceleration as an unqualified policy goal fails to account for this historical precedent.⏎⏎⏎⏎I have been a hospital CFO for fourteen years. I sat through the initial EHR rollout promises and watched alert fatigue from clinical decision support systems become so severe that clinicians developed systematic override habits that persist to this day. We lived through those unintended consequences on the operational side, and the financial wreckage from poorly integrated systems is real and quantifiable.⏎⏎⏎⏎Our internal analysis across 47 health system clients, representing more than 1.2 million AI-assisted echocardiogram reads conducted between Q1 2023 and Q3 2024, found reimbursement adjudication outcomes varying by 34 percentage points depending on the payer. This variance is driven by inconsistent classification of the same AI product as either a billable clinical decision support tool or a non-reimbursable administrative aid. A structured CFO survey conducted in Q4 2024 found that 61% of respondents had faced unplanned compliance expenditures averaging $280,000 per facility. It is important to note that this $280,000 figure only covers direct costs; it completely excludes the staff hours consumed by payer audit response workflows. In several client facilities, these audit efforts required hundreds of additional hours per quarter that remain untracked. This is a systemic failure condition that is hitting health system operating budgets directly.⏎⏎⏎⏎The academic literature has long documented these deployment risks. As early as 2004, informatics researchers were warning about the unintended consequences of health IT. More recently, studies have shown that performance degradation is further compounded by automation bias. Clinician over-reliance on algorithmic outputs removes the human correction that might otherwise catch AI errors (NEJM, 2017). Additionally, benchmark datasets simply do not replicate the real-world EHR data noise that undermines input quality, as Sendak and colleagues demonstrated in their 2020 computing research.⏎⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE ANY ACCELERATION⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS should condition any reimbursement pathway or deployment incentive on the following:⏎⏎⏎⏎1. CMS must require prospective real-world performance validation in actual deployment environments before making any coverage determinations.⏎⏎2. Independent post-deployment surveillance that uses standardized reporting metrics, completely replacing vendor-self-reported outcomes.⏎⏎3. Standardize classification frameworks across payers for AI-augmented diagnostic workflows. This is the only way to eliminate the adjudication variance our data has shown.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS must evaluate whether AI tools actually perform in the complex operational settings where they will run, not just in controlled demonstrations. That requires a different evidentiary standard than what current CMS coverage processes demand, and the agency has not yet proposed one.⏎⏎⏎⏎jmiller@enlitic.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Mitchell♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔Massachusetts Institute of Technology♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS must delay further clinical AI adoption until tort liability is explicitly allocated. The current principal-agent friction between software vendors and physicians generates unquantifiable malpractice risk and unpriced externalities (cf. Agrawal et al., NBER 2019). Clarifying indemnification frameworks ex ante is economically necessary before widespread hospital implementation, rather than relying on inefficient ex post litigation.⏎⏎Joseph Mitchell, Ph.D.⏎Health Economist, Massachusetts Institute of Technology⏎jmitchell@mit.edu♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
